-- 1362: Blog post -- "Creating Engaging Videos with Generative AI: Script, Shoot Less, Finish Faster".
-- Focus: the GenAI video workflow -- scripting/work plans, footage + audio improvement, object
-- removal, VFX, voice cloning, AI music -- and where human judgement still decides quality.
-- Funnels to WSQ Creating Engaging Videos with Generative AI (GenAI) (TGS-2024043855), SG-only.
-- Published live (status=1), BACK-DATED to 2025-07-11 per the admin's "date all the digital
-- marketing blogs in Apr to Aug 2025" (post 12 of 16, WSQ/CASL digital marketing batch).
--
-- BACK-DATED => linkedin_urn / facebook_post_id = 'manual-skip' so publishDue's share
-- step permanently no-ops (standing rule, blog-pipeline SKILL.md "Hard-won rules").
--
-- All external + course URLs verified HTTP 200 on 2026-09-07.
--
-- NOTE: hero_image_url is NULL here by design -- generate the hero ON PROD after
-- deploy. Hero artwork string (NOT the stored title, which would mis-theme):
--   artwork: Creating Engaging Videos with Generative AI: Script to Final Cut
--   kicker:  Video Marketing

-- @mms_instance is pre-set by apply.php from MMS_COUNTRY_CODE (defaults to 'SG').
SET @is_sg := IF(@mms_instance = 'SG', 1, 0);

INSERT INTO mmd_blog_post
  (title, url_key, excerpt, content, author, status, published_at, related_skus, source_sku,
   meta_title, meta_description, meta_keywords, likes, linkedin_urn, facebook_post_id,
   created_at, updated_at)
SELECT 'Creating Engaging Videos with Generative AI: Script, Shoot Less, Finish Faster', 'creating-engaging-videos-generative-ai-workflow', CONCAT('Video converts better than any other format and gets made least, because production is slow and expensive. Generative AI compresses every stage ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' scripting, footage cleanup, object removal, voice, music and VFX ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' so a small team can ship video weekly. Here is the workflow and where quality still needs a human eye. Plus the WSQ course, funded up to 70%.'),
  '<p><strong>Short version:</strong> Video outperforms every other content format on engagement and conversion, and it is the format small marketing teams produce least &mdash; because a two-minute video traditionally means scripting, filming, editing, audio work and several days that nobody has. Generative AI compresses each of those stages: scripts and work plans in minutes, footage and audio improved automatically, objects removed without frame-by-frame masking, voiceover and music generated rather than sourced. The result is that weekly video becomes realistic for a team of one or two. This article walks the workflow and names the parts where a human eye still decides whether it is any good. The hands-on version is the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-creating-engaging-videos-with-generative-ai-genai.html">WSQ Creating Engaging Videos with Generative AI</a> course (TGS-2024043855), funded up to 70%.</p> <h2>Why video gets skipped</h2> <p>Ask a small marketing team why they publish little video and the honest answer is rarely &ldquo;we do not think it works&rdquo;. It is that the production chain has too many steps, each requiring a different skill: writing for the ear, being comfortable on camera, editing, colour, audio, motion graphics. Any one weak link produces something that looks amateur next to competitors, so the safer choice is another blog post.</p> <p>Generative AI does not remove the need for judgement, but it removes the <em>skill floor</em> at several of those steps. Audio cleanup that used to need an engineer is now a button. Object removal that meant rotoscoping is now a selection. That changes which teams can realistically ship video, which is the actual barrier.</p> <h2>Stage one: script and plan</h2> <p>Most weak marketing video is weak before anything is filmed, because the script wandered. Generative AI is genuinely strong here, provided you ask for structure rather than words:</p> <pre>Write a 90-second script for a video introducing our WSQ course&#10;to SME owners who are curious about AI but sceptical it applies&#10;to them.&#10;&#10;Structure: hook in the first 5 seconds that names their doubt,&#10;the problem in their words, three concrete things they will be&#10;able to do after the course, one proof point, a clear next step.&#10;&#10;Constraints: spoken English, short sentences, no jargon, no&#10;"in today''s fast-paced world". Mark the b-roll needed beside&#10;each line. Flag any claim I need to verify.</pre> <p>Two details do most of the work. Asking for the hook to name the viewer''s doubt produces an opening that stops the scroll, and requesting b-roll notes alongside the script means the shot list writes itself. The course pairs scripting with work plans and timelines for exactly this reason &mdash; a script with a production plan attached gets made; a script alone often does not.</p> <h2>Stage two: footage you did not have to reshoot</h2> <p>The stage that saves the most calendar time is fixing what you already shot. Modern AI-powered platforms handle a set of tasks that used to send footage back for a reshoot:</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Old fix</th><th>AI-assisted fix</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Noisy or echoey audio</td><td>Reshoot, or an audio engineer</td><td>Enhancement in one pass &mdash; usually the single biggest quality lift</td></tr> <tr><td>Something distracting in shot</td><td>Reshoot or crop awkwardly</td><td>Object removal across the clip</td></tr> <tr><td>Inconsistent lighting or colour</td><td>Manual grading</td><td>Automatic matching across clips</td></tr> <tr><td>Long rambling takes</td><td>Manual scrubbing</td><td>Transcript-based editing &mdash; cut the text, the video follows</td></tr> <tr><td>No voiceover talent available</td><td>Hire, or record badly</td><td>Generated or cloned voice, with consent</td></tr> <tr><td>Music licensing</td><td>Library search and fees</td><td>Generated music to the required length and mood</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>If you adopt only one of these, adopt audio enhancement. Viewers forgive imperfect visuals and abandon bad sound, and it is consistently the cheapest quality improvement available.</p> <h2>Stage three: the parts AI does not decide</h2> <p>Three judgements remain stubbornly human, and they are the ones that separate a video that performs from one that merely exists:</p> <ul> <li><strong>The first three seconds.</strong> No tool knows what will stop your specific audience scrolling. Test openings against real viewers, not intuition.</li> <li><strong>Pace.</strong> AI-assisted edits tend toward evenness. Good video breathes &mdash; it lingers where the point lands and cuts hard where it does not.</li> <li><strong>Whether it is honest.</strong> Generated visuals, cloned voices and synthetic presenters raise real questions. Get consent for any cloned voice or likeness, do not fabricate testimonials or results, and keep claims within <a href="https://asas.org.sg/">Singapore advertising standards</a>. A video that misrepresents costs more than it earns.</li> </ul> <h2>Cutting one shoot into a month of assets</h2> <p>The economics of video improve dramatically when a single recording session feeds several formats. Because AI-assisted editing works from the transcript, slicing a long piece into short ones is a text operation rather than a timeline one &mdash; which is what makes this practical rather than theoretical.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Output</th><th>Where it comes from</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>The main video (60&ndash;120s)</td><td>The full script, edited</td><td>Website, YouTube, email</td></tr> <tr><td>Three to five short cuts (15&ndash;30s)</td><td>Single points lifted from the transcript</td><td>Short-form feeds and paid social</td></tr> <tr><td>A captioned silent version</td><td>Same edit, burnt-in captions</td><td>Feeds where sound is off by default</td></tr> <tr><td>An audio-only cut</td><td>The cleaned audio track</td><td>Podcast clip or voice note</td></tr> <tr><td>A written post</td><td>The transcript, restructured</td><td>Blog or newsletter, closing the loop back to text</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Two practical notes. Burn captions in rather than relying on platform auto-captions &mdash; a large share of feed video is watched muted, and the auto-generated versions mangle product names. And cut the short versions to stand alone: a clip that only makes sense to someone who saw the full video wastes the impression it earned.</p> <h2>A weekly cadence that is actually sustainable</h2> <p>The realistic target for a small team is one substantial video a week plus a few short cuts from it. A workable rhythm: script on Monday from the month''s content plan, record on Tuesday in one session for two or three videos, run the AI cleanup and edit on Wednesday, cut the short-form variants on Thursday, publish and engage on Friday. Batching the recording is the part that makes it survive a busy week &mdash; setting up once for three videos costs barely more than setting up for one.</p> <p>For the multi-agent version of this pipeline, where separate agents handle ideation, scripting, asset generation and quality assurance, see our post on <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/hermes-agent-kanban-multi-agent-video-team">running a multi-agent video team</a> and the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-agentic-ai-for-video-creation.html">WSQ Agentic AI for Video Creation</a> course.</p> <h2>Learn it hands-on, funded up to 70%</h2> <p>The <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-creating-engaging-videos-with-generative-ai-genai.html">WSQ &ndash; Creating Engaging Videos with Generative AI (GenAI)</a> course (TGS-2024043855) is a two-day, hands-on programme using tools including Sora, Runway and Veed.io. Its three topics:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Create and edit video scripts using generative AI</strong> &mdash; generating scripts, developing work plans and timelines, editing basics, and improving audio quality.</li> <li><strong>Improve video footage on GAI-powered video platforms</strong> &mdash; enhancing footage and overall production quality.</li> <li><strong>Apply new AI technologies to improve video production</strong> &mdash; object removal, adding VFX through AI analysis, voice cloning and creating music with generative AI.</li> </ol> <p>Assessment is a written and a practical exam. We also published a companion walkthrough on <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/create-engaging-videos-with-generative-ai">creating engaging videos with generative AI</a>.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Funding</th><th>What you pay (incl. 9% GST)</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Full course fee</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>S$1,000.00 before GST (S$90.00 GST)</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above</td><td>50% WSQ funding</td><td><strong>S$590.00</strong></td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored SG/PR staff</td><td>70% WSQ funding</td><td><strong>S$390.00</strong></td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Eligible Singaporeans can offset the nett fee with <a href="https://www.skillsfuture.gov.sg/">SkillsFuture Credit</a> or PSEA, and eligible Singapore-registered companies can tap SkillsFuture Enterprise Credit (SFEC); SME employers can also claim absentee payroll. The course runs over two full days (9:30am to 6:30pm), with physical classroom and synchronous online (Zoom) options. Fees are as published on the course page at the time of writing &mdash; the live page always carries the current fee and the next available dates.</p> <p>For short-form specifically, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-agentic-ai-for-tiktok-marketing.html">WSQ Agentic AI for TikTok Marketing</a> covers the platform mechanics; see the full <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-digital-marketing-courses.html">WSQ digital marketing range</a> for the rest.</p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Can AI make a whole marketing video for me?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>It can produce a competent draft of every component &mdash; script, voiceover, music, edit, effects &mdash; but the judgement calls that determine whether it performs remain human: the hook, the pacing, and whether the finished piece is honest and on-brand. Treat it as a very fast crew, not a director.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Which AI video improvement gives the biggest quality lift?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Audio enhancement, consistently. Viewers tolerate imperfect visuals and abandon bad sound within seconds, and cleanup that used to require an engineer now takes one pass.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is voice cloning acceptable in marketing videos?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Only with the explicit consent of the person whose voice it is. Cloning your own voice for narration is fine; cloning anyone else&rsquo;s without permission is not, and synthetic testimonials or fabricated results are off-limits under advertising standards regardless of how they were produced.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">How often should a small business publish video?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>One substantial video a week plus short cuts from it is a realistic and effective target. Batch the recording &mdash; setting up once for three videos costs barely more than for one, and batching is what makes the cadence survive a busy month.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need expensive equipment or software for the course?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. The course uses generative AI video platforms and the exercises are provided. Bring a laptop; a spare can be arranged. Good audio matters more than an expensive camera for most marketing video.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What funding applies?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>TGS-2024043855 is WSQ funded at 50% for Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above and 70% for Singaporeans aged 40 and above or SME-sponsored staff, with SkillsFuture Credit, PSEA and SFEC available against the nett fee.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>The bottom line</h2> <p>Video was skipped because the production chain demanded five different skills and several days. Generative AI lowers the floor on most of those steps &mdash; especially audio, cleanup and voice &mdash; which puts a weekly cadence within reach of a team of one or two. Write a script with a hook that names the viewer''s doubt, batch the recording, let AI do the repair work, and keep the hook, the pacing and the honesty firmly human.</p> <p><strong>Ready to ship video weekly?</strong> <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-creating-engaging-videos-with-generative-ai-genai.html">Register for WSQ &ndash; Creating Engaging Videos with Generative AI</a> &mdash; 2 days, funded up to 70%, SkillsFuture Credit claimable.</p>',
  'Dr. Alfred Ang', 1, '2025-07-11', 'TGS-2024043855', 'TGS-2024043855',
  'Creating Engaging Videos with Generative AI: The Full Workflow | WSQ Course',
  CONCAT('A practical generative AI video workflow ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' scripting and work plans, footage and audio enhancement, object removal, VFX, voice cloning and AI music ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' with the WSQ Creating Engaging Videos with Generative AI course (TGS-2024043855) funded up to 70%.'),
  'generative ai video creation, ai video production, ai video script, sora runway veed, ai video editing, voice cloning marketing, ai music generation, video marketing course singapore, wsq video course, skillsfuture video course, ai vfx, video content marketing',
  136, 'manual-skip', 'manual-skip', NOW(), NOW()
FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg = 1
